---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import AuthorByline from '../components/AuthorByline.astro';
import { BUSINESS } from '../data/site';

const title = 'Does Your Current Coverage Still Make Sense?';
const description = 'Medigap premiums rise over time. In New York and Connecticut you can reconsider your coverage without health questions — here is how to review it.';
const today = '2026-09-30';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    { '@type': 'Article', headline: title, description, author: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#agent` }, publisher: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#organization` }, datePublished: today, dateModified: today, mainEntityOfPage: `${BUSINESS.url}/does-your-coverage-still-make-sense` },
    { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/does-your-coverage-still-make-sense#webpage`, name: title, speakable: { '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification', cssSelector: ['.lead', 'h2'] } },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <PageHero
    title="Does Your Current Coverage Still Make Sense?"
    subtitle="Premiums change. Your needs change. A periodic review checks whether what you have — and what you pay for it — still fits."
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Does Your Current Coverage Still Make Sense?' }]}
  />

  <article class="py-14 lg:py-18">
    <div class="container-narrow prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:text-crst-primary prose-a:text-crst-accent">

      <p class="lead text-xl text-navy-700">
        Your premiums can increase over time. A plan that was a good value when you first enrolled may be considerably more
        expensive today.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-500 -mt-2">
        Written by <a href="/about">Jim Farnham, MS, MBA</a> — Licensed Agent / Insurance Broker (NY license {BUSINESS.nyLicense}).
      </p>

      <p>
        If you live in New York or Connecticut, you have an important advantage: state rules provide significant
        guaranteed-issue protections that generally allow you to change Medigap coverage without having to qualify based on
        your health.
      </p>
      <p>That makes it worthwhile to periodically ask a simple question:</p>
      <p><strong>Does the coverage I have—and the premium I’m paying for it—still make sense for me?</strong></p>

      <h2>Start With Your Current Coverage</h2>
      <p>The goal isn’t to change your plan simply because another option exists.</p>
      <p>First, look at what you have now:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What is your current monthly premium?</li>
        <li>How much has that premium increased since you enrolled?</li>
        <li>How much are you paying over the course of an entire year?</li>
        <li>How frequently do you actually use medical services?</li>
        <li>How much financial predictability do you want from your Medigap coverage?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For many people, a traditional Medigap plan remains an appropriate choice. The higher premium may be well worth the
        predictability and limited out-of-pocket exposure it provides.
      </p>
      <p>For others, the numbers may tell a different story.</p>

      <h2>Consider the Premium as Part of Your Healthcare Spending</h2>
      <p>
        It’s easy to think about a Medigap policy primarily in terms of what it pays when you receive medical care. But your
        premium is also a healthcare expense—and you pay it whether you use medical services or not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose you’re paying more each month for a traditional Medigap policy than you would for a high-deductible
        alternative. Over 12 months, that premium difference can become significant.
      </p>
      <p>The important comparison isn’t simply which plan pays more of your medical bills.</p>
      <p>It’s:</p>
      <p><strong>How much am I paying in premiums to avoid those potential out-of-pocket expenses?</strong></p>

      <h2>High-Deductible Plan G Offers a Different Approach</h2>
      <p>High-Deductible Plan G is designed around a different tradeoff.</p>
      <p>
        You generally pay a lower monthly premium, but you assume more of your Medicare-approved expenses yourself until you
        satisfy the plan’s annual high deductible.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the high deductible is satisfied, the policy provides the benefits of Plan G for the remainder of the calendar
        year.
      </p>
      <p>
        That doesn’t make High-Deductible Plan G better than traditional Plan G. And it certainly isn’t appropriate for
        everyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        But for someone who is comfortable assuming more of the smaller and routine expenses in exchange for lower premiums,
        it can be worth examining.
      </p>
      <p>A Medigap comparison should not begin with:</p>
      <p><em>“Which plan provides the most coverage?”</em> or <em>“Does this plan have deductible?”</em></p>
      <p>It should begin with:</p>
      <p><strong>“Which balance of premium, coverage and potential out-of-pocket expense makes the most sense for me?”</strong></p>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-white font-heading text-lg mb-2 m-0">Want a plain-English comparison of what you have now?</p>
        <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mb-4 m-0">A no-cost, no-pressure review by phone or Zoom screen share.</p>
        <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-base px-7 py-3">Call {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
      </div>

      <h2>New York and Connecticut Give You an Important Opportunity to Reconsider</h2>
      <p>This is where living in New York or Connecticut can make a meaningful difference.</p>
      <p>
        In many states, someone who wants to change Medigap policies after the initial Medigap Open Enrollment Period may
        have to go through medical underwriting and may not qualify for the new policy.
      </p>
      <p>New York and Connecticut provide broader guaranteed-issue protections.</p>
      <p>
        As a result, your health generally doesn’t prevent you from taking another look at your Medigap coverage simply
        because many years have passed since you first enrolled.
      </p>
      <p>That flexibility makes periodically reviewing your coverage particularly worthwhile.</p>

      <h2>A Review Doesn’t Mean You Have to Change Anything</h2>
      <p>Sometimes the best result of a Medicare review is discovering that you’re already in the right plan.</p>
      <p>
        Other times, the comparison may reveal an opportunity to reduce your premiums while maintaining a level of protection
        you’re comfortable with.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The objective isn’t to change your coverage. It’s to make sure you’re still comfortable with what you’re
        getting for what you’re paying.</strong>
      </p>

      <h2>See Whether Your Current Coverage Still Makes Sense</h2>
      <p>
        I can compare your existing Medigap coverage with the alternatives available to you and explain the differences in
        plain English—including the potential savings and the additional financial responsibility that can come with a
        high-deductible approach.
      </p>
      <p>Then you can decide whether staying exactly where you are or making a change makes more sense.</p>
      <p>
        Learn more about <a href="/services/high-deductible-medicare-supplement">High-Deductible Medicare Supplement Insurance</a>
        or <a href="/services/medicare-supplement">traditional Medigap</a>.
      </p>

      <AuthorByline />

      <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <Disclaimer variant="tpmo" />
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          This page is educational and general; plan benefits, costs, and availability vary by insurer and location and are not
          guaranteed. NY producer license #: {BUSINESS.nyLicense}.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-2 leading-relaxed">
          Source: <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Medicare.gov — Medicare Supplement (Medigap)</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection headline="Already on Medicare? Let’s see whether your coverage still makes sense." />
</BaseLayout>
